    "moment": [
      {
        "kind": "quote",
        "text": "Commonly, contemporary portraits of women were commissioned for either of two occasions: betrothal or marriage.",
        "source": {
          "author": "Wikipedia contributors",
          "title": "Ginevra de' Benci",
          "readAt": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ginevra_de'_Benci",
          "accessed": "2026-11-03",
          "tier": 5,
          "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "quote",
        "text": "In Renaissance Italy, the juniper was regarded a symbol of female virtue, while the Italian word for juniper, ginepro, also makes a play on Ginevra's name.",
        "source": {
          "author": "Wikipedia contributors",
          "title": "Ginevra de' Benci",
          "readAt": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ginevra_de'_Benci",
          "accessed": "2026-11-03",
          "tier": 5,
          "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
        }
      }
    ],
    "matters": [
      {
        "kind": "quote",
        "text": "The phrase is understood as symbolizing the intricate relationship between Ginevra's intellectual and moral virtue on the one hand, and her physical beauty on the other.",
        "lead": "On the Latin motto painted on the back:",
        "source": {
          "author": "Wikipedia contributors",
          "title": "Ginevra de' Benci",
          "readAt": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ginevra_de'_Benci",
          "accessed": "2026-11-03",
          "tier": 5,
          "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "quote",
        "text": "Ginevra is beautiful, but austere; she has no hint of a smile and her gaze, although forward, seems indifferent to the viewer.",
        "source": {
          "author": "Wikipedia contributors",
          "title": "Ginevra de' Benci",
